## The idea

Doing a search from every cell would be far too slow. Instead put **every supply point into the queue at the same time** with distance 0, then run one breadth-first search. The search spreads out like a wave from all supply points together, so the first time it reaches a cell, that distance is the distance to the **nearest** supply point. Walls are never entered; cells the wave never reaches stay −1.

## Step by step

1. Make a distance grid filled with −1.
2. Every "S" cell gets distance 0 and goes into the queue.
3. While the queue is not empty: take a cell; for each of its 4 neighbours that is inside the grid, not a wall and still −1, set distance = this cell's distance + 1 and add it to the queue.
4. WorstCase: the largest value in the distance grid (0 if nothing is reachable).

## Worked example

**Input:** S.. / .#. / ..S

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| start | queue: (0,0) and (2,2), both distance 0 |
| wave 1 | (0,1), (1,0), (1,2), (2,1) get distance 1 |
| wave 2 | (0,2) and (2,0) get distance 2; the wall (1,1) is never entered |

**Result:** 0,1,2 | 1,-1,1 | 2,1,0  (worst case 2)

## Cost

O(rows × cols) time and memory — every cell is queued at most once.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// 'S' = supply point, '#' = wall, '.' = open.
public static class Supply {

    // Steps from every cell to its nearest supply point; -1 for walls and unreachable cells.
    public static int[,] Distances(string[] grid) {
        if (grid == null || grid.Length == 0) return new int[0, 0];
        int rows = grid.Length, cols = grid[0].Length;
        int[,] distance = new int[rows, cols];
        Queue<int[]> queue = new Queue<int[]>();

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                distance[r, c] = -1;
                if (grid[r][c] == 'S') {                // ALL supply points start together
                    distance[r, c] = 0;
                    queue.Enqueue(new int[] { r, c });
                }
            }
        }

        int[] dRow = { 1, -1, 0, 0 };
        int[] dCol = { 0, 0, 1, -1 };
        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            int[] cell = queue.Dequeue();
            for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
                int nr = cell[0] + dRow[k];
                int nc = cell[1] + dCol[k];
                if (nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols) continue;   // outside
                if (grid[nr][nc] == '#') continue;                             // wall
                if (distance[nr, nc] != -1) continue;                          // already reached
                distance[nr, nc] = distance[cell[0], cell[1]] + 1;
                queue.Enqueue(new int[] { nr, nc });
            }
        }
        return distance;
    }

    // How far the worst-served reachable cell is from supply.
    public static int WorstCase(string[] grid) {
        int[,] distance = Distances(grid);
        int worst = 0;
        foreach (int d in distance) {
            if (d > worst) worst = d;
        }
        return worst;
    }
}
```
